{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>You shall surely set a king over you.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this is a positive commandment, that there be a king in Israel. And Israel were commanded in three commandments when they entered the Land: to appoint for themselves a king, as it is written, שום תשים עליך מלך; to eradicate the seed of Amalek, as it is written (Deuteronomy 25:19): ״והיה בהניח ה׳ אלהיך לך מכל אויביך״ — and so forth — ״תמחה את זכר עמלק מתחת השמים״; to build the House of Choice, as it is written (Deuteronomy 12:5): ״לשכנו תדרשו ובאת שמה״. The appointment of the king precedes the eradication of Amalek, as it is written (I Samuel 15:1): ״אותי שלח ה׳ למשחך למלך״ — and afterward — ״עתה לך והכיתה את עמלק״. And the eradication of Amalek precedes the building of the House, as it is written (II Samuel 7:1): ״ויהי כי ישב המלך בביתו וה׳ הניח לו מסביב, ויאמר המלך אל נתן הנביא ראה אנכי יושב בבית ארזים וארון האלהים יושב בתוך היריעה״. Now this commandment is according to the will of Israel, that they are destined to ask for him; not according to the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, for He, exalted be He, is the King who goes in the midst of their camp, watching over each and every one of them, and this chosen people — HaShem of all is their King. And therefore it was grievous in His eyes, exalted be He, when they asked for him from Samuel the prophet — that is what He said to him (I Samuel 8:7): ״כי לא אותך מאסו כי אותי מאסו ממלוך עליהם״. And He gave them Saul as king, not by His will or desire; as it is written (Hosea 8:4): ״הם המליכו ולא ממני השירו ולא ידעתי״; and it is written (Hosea 13:11): ״אתן לך מלך באפי ואקח בעברתי״ — ״I will give you a king in My anger״ — this is Saul, the first king; ״and I will take him away in My wrath״ — this is Zedekiah, the last king, who was taken by the hand of Nebuchadnezzar in the wrath of the Holy One, blessed be He, and what happened to him happened. Go forth and learn what befell us at the hands of kings — as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: Saul fell at Mount Gilboa; David was given a plague, as it is written (II Samuel 24:15): ״ויתן ה׳ דבר בישראל״; Ahab held back the rains from them, as it is written (I Kings 17:1): ״אם יהיה השנים האלה״ — and so forth; Zedekiah destroyed the Temple. And that which all Israel agreed upon and asked for — thus they said in tractate Sanhedrin: the ignorant masses corrupted it, for they asked for him for their wars, as it is written (I Samuel 8:20): ״ויצא לפנינו ונלחם את מלחמותינו״; but the elders among them asked for him to be their judge and to discipline the stubborn among them." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״שום תשים עליך מלך״ — that his awe be upon you. No one may ride his horse, nor sit on his throne, nor use his sceptre, nor his crown, nor any of his utensils; and even if he waives his honor, his honor is not waived. And when he dies, his utensils are burned with him. Likewise they may not use his male slaves or female slaves — except for another king. Therefore Abishag the Shunamite was permitted to Solomon and forbidden to Adonijah; but the wife of a king is forbidden to another forever. Even a prophet stands before the king and prostrates himself to the ground, as it is written (I Kings 1:23): ״הנה נתן הנביא ויבא לפני המלך וישתחו למלך על אפיו ארצה״. And the king stands before the High Priest at the time he needs to inquire by the judgment of the Urim, as it is written (Numbers 27:21): ״ולפני אלעזר הכהן יעמוד ושאל לו במשפט האורים״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״שום תשים עליך מלך״ — know what the power of the king is: for it is the fourth leg of the Chariot, which is called Malkhut. For the crowns together with the Arbitrator are the three patriarchs of the Chariot, and the Malkhut is the fourth, and corresponding to it is the king of Israel. And the Malkhut is called ״צדק״, and the full verse is (Isaiah 32:1): ״הן לצדק ימלך מלך״. And concerning it, it is said (Psalms 118:22): ״אבן מאסו הבונים היתה לראש פנה״ — for it is the stone that completes the building. And ״the builders who despised it״ are the patriarchs, from whom the entire world is built — for each one took his measure from above, beyond it. And therefore David came and took it as his portion, and ״it became the chief cornerstone״, for it is the chief stone and the fourth corner. And even though the King is above the Malkhut, we find in places where the Malkhut is called ״king״ — as it is written (Isaiah 44:6): ״מלך ישראל וגואלו ה׳ צבאות״, and likewise (Psalms 145:1): ״ארוממך אלוהי המלך״ — this is said concerning the Malkhut, and this is the language of ״ארוממך״, and this is why it is written אלוהי מלא with a ו״ו. And you will find in Sefer Yetzirah: ״פנה עשירית וחתמו בו העשרה״ — the tenth corner, and with it He sealed the ten. And if so, it is obligatory to appoint a king in Israel, provided that their intention be for the sake of Heaven, toward the intention of the commandment — and this is clear. And the language of שום — He could have said תמנה or תקים, but it is from the language of סם (remedy), coming to hint that the king is a remedy of life, for he is included in all; and this is the sense of שום תשים — and all depends on the intention with which they ask for him." "<b>Whom HaShem your Elohim chooses.</b> This comes to teach that the kingship and likewise all the other ranks — all of them are from Heaven, and that choice was from the Most High. And thus our Rabbis of blessed memory said: even the head of a dung-heap is installed from Heaven. And thus Scripture says (Daniel 5:21): ״די שליט עילאה במלכות אנשא ולמאן די יצבא יתניניה״." "<b>From among your brothers you shall set a king over you.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: on condition that his father and mother be from Israel — and this is the language of ״מקרב אחיך״. And from here one may learn that anyone who is not your brother is not an Israelite, and anyone who is not an Israelite is called a foreigner."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :register {:fixes 1}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:23.686573836Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}